# Lecture 14E

## Chi-Square Test (&chi;^2^) for Hardy-Weinberg

## Chapter 10

## Dr. Neil Voss

## December 8, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Calculate expected genotype counts from allele frequencies.
- Complete a chi-square calculation for population data.
- Choose degrees of freedom for the worked examples.
- Interpret observed differences from Hardy-Weinberg expectations.

=== layout: section

# Hardy-Weinberg and Chi-square test

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Predicting Genotype Frequencies*

@body

- With the Hardy-Weinberg principle, allele frequencies can be used to predict the genotype frequencies.
- For the MN blood type example,
  - p = 0.5395 and q = 0.4605
- The predicted genotype frequencies are
    - LMLM	p2 = (0.5395)2 = 0.2911
    - LMLN	2pq = 2 (0.5395) (0.4605) = 0.4968
    - LNLN	q2 = (0.4605)2 = 0.2121

![Slide image 1](assets/lect14e-hardy_weinberg_chi_square/image_001.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Do these predictions fit the data?
- First we must calculate the predicted genotype numbers by multiplying the Hardy-Weinberg frequencies by the sample size (6129).
  - [*Genotype	Predicted Number*]{underline=true}
  - LMLM					0.2911 &#61620; 6129 = 1784.2
  - LMLN		0.4968 &#61620; 6129 = 3044.8
  - LNLN		0.2121 &#61620; 6129 = 1300.0

